+# Decision: Transport for delivering committed changes to browsers
+
+## Context
+
+Each event keeps one authoritative board. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the
+server assigns each committed edit an event revision; those revisions then have to reach
+up to 800 simultaneous browsers per event, within 15 seconds under normal connectivity,
+with subsecond updates useful but not required. The transport that carries revisions to
+browsers is the hinge the rest of the system hangs from: the change store, the delivery
+component, and browser sync are all sized around it — a polling transport wants cursor
+queries and a simple client loop, a push transport wants replayable revisions, session
+management, and client-side reconnect/catch-up. Choosing wrong is expensive to unwind
+because three unwritten nodes inherit the shape.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate separates the candidates: all three transports can meet the 15-second freshness
+target and all three preserve one authoritative revision per committed edit, so freshness
+and authority are background, not selectors.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Vendor independence** (major) — whether the board's realtime path couples it to a
+  third-party provider. Load-bearing because a coordinator-run market board should not
+  take a dependency it cannot operate or replace itself.
+- **Update latency** (moderate) — how quickly a committed revision becomes visible.
+  15 seconds is required; faster is useful operationally but not required.
+- **Recurring operating cost** (moderate) — ongoing cost the transport imposes, whether
+  as provider fees or as read/compute amplification across 800 browsers.
+- **Connection & reconnect operations** (moderate) — connection capacity, session
+  lifecycle, and reconnect/catch-up correctness the team must own and run.
+- **Downstream design load** (minor) — the machinery the transport forces onto the change
+  store and browser sync.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board every ~5 seconds over ordinary HTTPS,
+asking for changes since a cursor they hold. No persistent connections exist; delivery
+stays plain request/response. The change store exposes cursor queries over its revision
+history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — no third party in the path.
+- Update latency: **partial** — a revision surfaces on the next poll, so visible lag runs
+  up to the poll interval; meets the 15-second target with no subsecond headroom.
+- Recurring operating cost: **partial** — no provider fee, but read volume is 800 browsers
+  polling on a fixed interval regardless of whether anything changed.
+- Connection & reconnect operations: **strong** — stateless; nothing is kept alive, so
+  there are no sessions to manage and no reconnect path to get right.
+- Downstream design load: **strong** — the store needs cursor queries; browsers run a
+  simple poll loop; no replay or reconnect machinery.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest and statelessly operated, paid for in multi-second lag and repeated
+reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server holds persistent WebSocket connections it operates itself and
+pushes each committed revision to the subscribed browsers. The change store keeps
+replayable revision history and reliably publishes each committed revision to delivery;
+delivery owns socket sessions — subscription, fan-out, and connection lifecycle; browsers
+hold a subscription and run reconnect and catch-up to recover revisions missed while
+disconnected.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — no third-party realtime provider; the transport runs
+  on infrastructure the team operates.
+- Update latency: **strong** — revisions are pushed as they commit; subsecond is
+  achievable.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no provider fee; the cost is the team's own
+  compute for held connections rather than a per-message or per-connection bill.
+- Connection & reconnect operations: **weak** — the team owns connection capacity for 800
+  simultaneous browsers and all reconnect/catch-up correctness, including opening-time
+  bursts.
+- Downstream design load: **partial** — the store must provide replayable revisions and a
+  reliable publication path; browsers must implement reconnect and catch-up.
+
+**Summary.** Fast and vendor-free, bought with the connection and reconnect operations the
+team now owns.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider carries revisions to browsers. Delivery
+wraps the provider's publish API and reconciles each committed revision onto it; browsers
+subscribe through the provider's client SDK.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — the realtime path is coupled to the provider's API,
+  SDK, and availability.
+- Update latency: **strong** — low-latency push, typically subsecond.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — recurring fees that scale with connections and
+  messages.
+- Connection & reconnect operations: **strong** — the provider owns connection capacity
+  and reconnect handling.
+- Downstream design load: **partial** — the store needs publication reconciliation against
+  the provider; browsers adopt the provider's subscription model; the least connection
+  code the team writes itself.
+
+**Summary.** Lowest-latency with the least connection operations, bought with recurring
+fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+On the major criterion, vendor independence, polling and self-hosted sockets tie and the
+hosted service is the outlier — it is the only option that couples the board's realtime
+path to a provider, and a market board the coordinators run themselves should keep that
+path replaceable. That removes the hosted service despite it being strongest on both
+update latency and connection operations.
+
+Between the two vendor-free options, self-hosted sockets beat polling on update latency
+(push versus up-to-interval lag) and on recurring operating cost (committed-revision pushes
+versus 800 browsers reading on a fixed interval whether or not anything changed). The price
+is the connection & reconnect operations criterion, where sockets are weak and polling is
+strong: the team takes on connection capacity and reconnect/catch-up correctness. That cost
+is accepted deliberately to get fast, vendor-free updates — it is the live tradeoff this
+decision makes, not an oversight.
+
+This decision turns on **vendor independence** — keeping the realtime path on
+infrastructure the team operates rather than a third-party realtime vendor.
+
+Committing forecloses adopting a provider subscription SDK on the browser and a
+provider-reconciled publication path in the store. It obligates downstream: the change
+store must retain replayable revision history and a reliable path that publishes each
+committed revision to delivery; delivery must own socket session management, revision
+fan-out, and connection lifecycle including opening-time bursts; browser sync must
+implement reconnect and catch-up against revisions missed while disconnected.
